# Copy to .env and adjust for your environment. config.Load() automatically
# loads a .env found in the working directory or any of its parents (real
# environment variables always win over .env values -- see internal/config/
# config.go), so `go run ./cmd/server` picks this up with no exporting needed.
# Docker Compose (deploy/docker-compose.yml) does NOT read this file: it sets its
# own values for the static-IP bridge network it creates. This .env is for running
# cmd/server directly against a locally-installed Asterisk.

# ARI REST base URL. The events WebSocket URL is derived from it automatically
# (http -> ws, https -> wss, + /events).
ARI_URL=http://localhost:8088/ari
ARI_USER=vaani
ARI_PASS=vaani
# Stasis application name Asterisk routes calls into (deploy/asterisk/extensions.conf).
ARI_APP=vaani

# Address advertised to Asterisk as externalMedia's external_host. Required,
# never auto-detected -- see the MEDIA_IP decision documented in
# internal/config/config.go. 127.0.0.1 only makes sense when Asterisk and this
# binary run on the same host outside Docker.
MEDIA_IP=127.0.0.1
MEDIA_PORT_BASE=20000
MEDIA_PORT_COUNT=256

# externalMedia transport: "rtp" (default, UDP) or "audiosocket" (TCP, Asterisk's
# res_audiosocket protocol -- see internal/media/audiosocket.go). AudioSocket has
# no jitter buffer or endianness question of its own (TCP already guarantees
# order, and its payload is little-endian by protocol definition), so
# AUDIO_L16_ENDIANNESS/JITTER_BUFFER_PACKETS below are ignored when this is set
# to "audiosocket". UDP tends to be more fragile across routed/firewalled network
# paths than TCP; audiosocket is worth trying if RTP calls won't reliably connect
# across sites.
MEDIA_ENCAPSULATION=rtp

# Prometheus /metrics listen address.
METRICS_ADDR=:9091

# Wire byte order of inbound RTP L16 payloads: "le" or "be". Defaults to "le" if
# unset, but that default is unverified until you've actually run
# `go run ./cmd/endianness-check` against a live stack -- until then the server
# logs a startup warning. See docs/AUDIO_PIPELINE.md.
#
# This has been observed to differ by Asterisk build (one deployment needed "be",
# confirmed live: "le" decoded to noise, "be" decoded to real speech) -- treat it
# as a per-deployment fact to verify, never copy another deployment's value.
AUDIO_L16_ENDIANNESS=le

# Jitter buffer window size in 20ms packets (1-10; default 3 = 60ms). Also sets
# the priming depth before any real audio is released. See docs/AUDIO_PIPELINE.md.
JITTER_BUFFER_PACKETS=3

# If set, enables per-call debug WAV recording (both directions,
# post-normalization) under this directory. Leave empty to disable.
RECORD_DIR=

# Per-call Handler: "loopback" (default, echoes audio back) or "agent" (the
# voice agent; needs the Dograh settings below).
APP_MODE=loopback

# Debug hook: forces every call's Handler to produce zero audio frames, so the
# write tick's silence-fallback path fires on every single tick. Never set this
# in production -- calls carry no audio at all. Leave unset/0 normally.
TEST_SILENT_HANDLER=0

# Enables the live /debug/audio/{callID} tap on METRICS_ADDR: GET it while a call
# is active to stream that call's inbound audio (raw s16le PCM, post-
# normalization) in real time, e.g.:
#   curl -sN http://localhost:9091/debug/audio/<callID> | aplay -f S16_LE -r 16000 -c1 -
# Never set this in production -- anyone who can reach METRICS_ADDR can listen to
# live call audio. Leave 0 normally.
DEBUG_AUDIO=0

# Backstop cap on how long a single call may live before Vaani hangs it up,
# however healthy it looks -- protects against a wedged call burning billable
# telephony time forever. 0 disables the cap.
MAX_CALL_DURATION_SECONDS=0

# How many seconds of completely silent inbound media (zero audio, not just
# "quiet") before Vaani proactively hangs the call up -- a second, independent
# layer alongside Asterisk's own rtptimeout/rtpholdtimeout
# (deploy/asterisk/rtp.conf), which only watches the caller's SIP-side RTP and
# can't catch a call whose audio never reaches this externalMedia leg due to an
# Asterisk-internal bridging problem. See docs/AUDIO_PIPELINE.md. On by default
# (unlike MAX_CALL_DURATION_SECONDS): 0 disables it.
MEDIA_DEAD_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=30

# --- Phase 3 (agent) settings -----------------------------------------------
# Set APP_MODE=agent above to route calls through internal/ai/agent.Handler
# (Sarvam STT -> LLM -> Sarvam TTS with local barge-in) instead of
# LoopbackHandler. Leave APP_MODE=loopback (above) for telephony-only testing,
# e.g. SIPp load tests: it has no AI dependency at all.

# The agent runs a Dograh workflow, read straight from Dograh's Postgres (the
# same DB its editor writes to): prompts, greeting, nodes, edges, tools, and
# the LLM/STT/TTS models and keys (the workflow owner's model configuration,
# with the workflow's model overrides on top -- as in Dograh). Nothing about
# the models is set here. Both required in agent mode; unreachable DB =
# startup fails.
#   postgresql://user:pass@host:5432/dograh
DOGRAH_DB_URL=
# The workflow's number, as in its editor URL (/workflow/19). Its published
# version is read fresh on every call, so a publish applies to the next call;
# if it can't be used (missing, unsupported provider), the call is hung up.
# Supported: STT/TTS provider sarvam; any OpenAI-compatible LLM provider
# (openai, google, groq, openrouter, sarvam, bifrost, speaches). Tool
# categories: end_call, transfer_call, http_api (with its credential from
# Dograh); others skipped with a warning.
DOGRAH_WORKFLOW_ID=

# Caller-interrupts-agent (barge-in) master switch. 1 = on: while the agent
# speaks, the barge-in detector watches the caller's inbound audio and a
# sustained interrupt cuts playback so the caller's words become the next turn
# -- but only at workflow nodes whose "allow_interrupt" is on in Dograh; at
# the others the caller's speech is ignored until the agent finishes, as in
# Dograh. 0 = never cut, whatever the nodes say: with VAD_MODE=ten the VAD
# still runs and its speech started/ended lines keep logging (observe-only);
# with VAD_MODE=energy this is a full no-op. Read at startup -- changing it
# requires a restart.
BARGE_IN_ENABLED=1
# RMS speech threshold (media.RMS units, int16 PCM scale). Only used when
# VAD_MODE=energy. 500 is an untuned starting guess, not a verified number --
# like AUDIO_L16_ENDIANNESS, calibrate it against real call audio before
# trusting it in production.
BARGE_IN_RMS_FLOOR=500
# Ignore barge-in detection for this long after the agent starts speaking, so
# its own voice leaking into the mic can't immediately self-trigger.
BARGE_IN_GUARD_MS=300
# How long the caller must keep speaking, without a break, before it counts as
# an interruption. 200 matches Dograh (its VAD's start_secs 0.2). Raise it for
# noisy lines; shorter bursts (a cough, a word in the background) never cut.
BARGE_IN_MIN_SPEECH_MS=200
# On by default (400); 0 turns it off. Once TEN VAD (VAD_MODE=ten; without it
# nothing changes) has heard this many ms of quiet after the caller spoke,
# ask Sarvam to finalize the
# transcript now ({"type":"flush"} on a flush_signal=true connection) instead
# of waiting for Sarvam's own end-of-speech, which takes ~600-700ms -- the
# largest part of the reply delay. Dograh does this on its local VAD's 0.2s
# stop. Confirmed working live at 400 (2026-10-03/05): Sarvam answers each
# flush with its end-of-speech signal within ~30-90ms, then the transcript,
# so end_detect_ms drops from ~600-700 to ~440-520 and the reply comes
# ~0.2-0.3s sooner. Log: "stt flush sent", then since_flush_ms= on the
# transcript. If a caller's sentence gets split ("transcript ignored: the
# agent is thinking" right after a short [User] line), raise it or set 0.
STT_FLUSH_AFTER_MS=400
# An interruption first PAUSES the reply. A transcript confirms it (the reply
# is cut and the caller's words become the next turn); with no transcript and
# 2s of quiet it was noise, and the reply resumes from the start of the
# sentence it stopped in. This is how long after the pause transcripts are
# ignored -- one arriving that soon was already on its way, not what the
# caller is saying now. Keep it short: 1200 (the old default) was seen live
# throwing away callers' real short answers. Junk transcripts (a lone letter
# such as "ह") are ignored whenever they arrive.
POST_CUT_SILENCE_MS=300
# Barge-in detector: "energy" (default -- RMS threshold, no native dependency)
# or "ten" (TEN VAD, a real neural VAD that tells speech from coughs/music/line
# noise). "ten" is Linux/cgo only (native library vendored under
# third_party/ten-vad); on any other platform -- or if the library can't be
# loaded -- calls fall back to the energy detector with a loud warning.
# With "ten" the VAD also runs while the agent is listening, so the logs can
# time the caller's last sound (end_detect_ms, since_last_speech_ms); its
# speech started/ended lines then appear while listening too.
VAD_MODE=energy
# TEN VAD speech-probability threshold [0,1] (the native decision is
# probability >= threshold). Default 0.7, the confidence Dograh's VAD runs
# with; TEN VAD's own example uses 0.5 (more sensitive). Only meaningful when
# VAD_MODE=ten.
TEN_VAD_THRESHOLD=0.7
